    "result_text": "Green. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephones in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline.\n\n**Change:** `NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java` \u2014 after the existing normalization (strip formatting, apply `+61` default, and check the 8\u201315 total-digit E.164 constraint), it now checks the national-number length against the country code:\n\n- `+61` \u2192 requires exactly **9** national digits\n- `+1` \u2192 requires exactly **10** national digits\n\nA `LinkedHashMap` holds the country-code \u2192 national-length rules with longest prefix first (`\"61\"` before `\"1\"`) so the code match is unambiguous. When the national number is the wrong length for its country, the step throws the existing checked `InvalidTelephoneException`, which its handler already maps to a **400** with `{\"errors\":[\"telephone\"]}`. Country codes without a rule fall through to the general 8\u201315 digit check unchanged.\n\n**Verification:**\n- The new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes.\n- The valid `ownerNode()` telephone (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still passes.\n- Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.\n\nI kept the validation inside the existing normalize step since it already owned the E.164 digit-count check, keeping the telephone rules cohesive in one place rather than adding a separate pipeline step.",
